      "notes": "All five claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate: the \u00a33.6bn pledge is correctly labelled projected (a government commitment, not a verified outturn); the \u00a32.3bn council spend is measurable from Resolution Foundation data; the prevention-cost-effectiveness claim is correctly projected and attributed to Platfform (an advocacy/sector source, appropriately labelled). The too-uncertain direction is genuinely supported by claims on both sides \u2014 upside (prevention savings) and downside (unfunded spending risk) \u2014 with no OBR/IFS costing in the evidence base. The crux_parameter and estimate_range are concrete and non-lazy. Magnitude and time_horizon are both n/a, consistent with too-uncertain. No lever leakage: the verdict judges fiscal effect, not homelessness policy merit per se. No party identity or motive attribution in the rationale. No false balance: the uncertainty is structural (missing fiscal architecture), not manufactured. One minor observation: E44 (Shelter quote via greaterchange.co.uk, a media/sector source) is the sole basis for the 'existing funding insufficient' claim; it is correctly labelled projected and is used only to illustrate spending pressure risk, not to drive the direction verdict \u2014 acceptable under the rules. No blocking issues found."
